WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.297
I'm a low key, I won't be soon In New York City, when I see you.
我很低调，不会很快在纽约见到你。

00:00:11.297 --> 00:00:18.359
Please permit me to tell you everything.
请允许我告诉你一切。

00:00:18.359 --> 00:00:21.359
In New York City.
在纽约市。

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:25.655
Well hello out there and welcome to another episode of How We Made Your Mother.
大家好，欢迎收看《我们如何创造了你的母亲》的另一集。

00:00:25.655 --> 00:00:26.525
I'm Josh Radner.
我是乔什·拉德纳。

00:00:26.525 --> 00:00:28.156
I'm joined by my friend Craig.
我的朋友克雷格也加入了我的行列。

00:00:28.156 --> 00:00:29.352
Tell him to say Craig.
告诉他说克雷格。

00:00:29.352 --> 00:00:30.168
Hey there Josh.
嘿，乔什。

00:00:30.168 --> 00:00:33.539
Hey we're uh we're still talking about How I Made Your Mother.
嘿，我们呃，我们还在谈论《我是如何造就你母亲的》。

00:00:33.539 --> 00:00:35.659
That's a TV show that we made together.
那是我们一起制作的电视节目。

00:00:35.659 --> 00:00:39.775
Craig co-created the show with his college buddy Carter Bayes.
克雷格与他的大学好友卡特·贝叶斯共同创作了该节目。

00:00:39.775 --> 00:00:41.899
I played Ted Mosby on that show.
我在那场演出中扮演泰德·莫斯比。

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:43.751
It ran from 2005 to 2014.
它从2005年持续到2014年。

00:00:43.751 --> 00:00:46.194
Today we're deep into season two.
今天我们已经深入了解第二季了。

00:00:46.194 --> 00:00:51.525
We're talking about the episode Columns written by our friend Matt Coon.
我们正在谈论由我们的朋友马特·库恩（Matt Coon）撰写的剧集《专栏》。

00:00:51.525 --> 00:00:57.819
Alec, give us a little history, place us in the constellation when did this premiere?
Alec，给我们讲一下历史，把我们放在星座中，这个首映是什么时候？

00:00:57.819 --> 00:00:58.633
Absolutely.
绝对地。

00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:13.960
Columns premiered on the television on January 22nd of 2007 television was like this thing and it was a new thing at that point I believe Yeah, see I was doing a new invention watching with their TV dinners.
专栏于 2007 年 1 月 22 日在电视上首播，电视就像这样，在那时它是一件新事物，我相信是的，看到我正在做一项新发明，一边看他们的电视晚餐。

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:29.065
I love Lucy was our lead in So uh, so this episode I gotta say when it was on the schedule, it's like, oh, we're talking about columns I was like, yeah, what the hell is a little bit of a stealth episode.
我喜欢露西是我们的主角，所以呃，所以这一集我必须说，当它被列入日程时，就像，哦，我们正在谈论专栏，我当时想，是的，到底是什么有点秘密剧集。

00:01:29.065 --> 00:01:34.100
I did not remember it It doesn't have a title that jogged my memory.
我不记得了 它没有一个唤起我记忆的标题。

00:01:34.099 --> 00:01:35.700
Yeah, and I was kind of like, what is this?
是的，我当时就想，这是什么？

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.739
Once it started, I was like, okay, I know what this is.
一旦开始，我就想，好吧，我知道这是什么。

00:01:38.739 --> 00:01:42.379
A couple of things I completely forgot, which were funny to me that I want to talk about.
有几件事我完全忘记了，对我来说很有趣，我想谈谈。

00:01:42.379 --> 00:01:50.180
But give us a little, as you do Craig, so beautifully, a little summary of what we just want.
但是，就像你对克雷格所做的那样，请给我们一点我们想要的东西的总结。

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:55.019
Yeah, this is how lucky are we that Brian Cranston came back and played with us again.
是的，布莱恩·克兰斯顿再次回来和我们一起比赛，我们真是太幸运了。

00:01:55.019 --> 00:01:59.099
He was an Aldrin Justice, and he came back a few episodes later into this episode.
他是奥尔德林法官，在本集几集后他又回来了。

00:01:59.099 --> 00:02:05.180
And he plays Ted's formerly asshole boss who Ted has supplanted and Ted is now the boss.
他扮演泰德以前的混蛋老板，泰德已经取代了他，泰德现在是老板。

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.659
And it's an investigation of kind of what it's like to be the boss.
这是对当老板的感觉的调查。

00:02:08.659 --> 00:02:12.060
Like Ted used to be the guy making fun of the boss, now he is the boss.
就像泰德曾经是那个取笑老板的人一样，现在他是老板。

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:19.139
Not only that, but now him and brothers is this completely antagonistic, bitter employee whom he has to manage.
不仅如此，现在他和兄弟们还是这个完全对立、苦毒的员工，他必须管理。

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:27.500
And then this is one of the more A-story B-stories him and episodes we've ever done where it really is like, it's almost like a little bit like a different show.
这是他和我们做过的更多 A 故事 B 故事之一，它真的就像是，它几乎有点像一个不同的节目。

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:39.620
The B story, very separate from the A story, which is not always the case on our show, is about this painting, a nude painting of Marshall's services and causes all kinds of havoc between Marshall, Barney, and Lily.
B 故事与 A 故事非常不同，在我们的节目中并不总是如此，B 故事是关于这幅画的，这是一幅马歇尔服务的裸体画，并在马歇尔、巴尼和莉莉之间造成了各种破坏。

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.300
Yeah, yeah.
是啊是啊。

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:49.659
Well, also one of the things, it's not just Hammond, but I really like the kind of grace notes of Ted is not, he's not a water cooler guy anymore.
好吧，还有一件事，这不仅仅是哈蒙德，但我真的很喜欢特德的那种装饰音，他不再是一个饮水机家伙了。

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:54.659
He's not like his other, his co-workers, he's gone into, is it the C suite?
他不像他的其他人，他的同事，他进入的是C 级主管吗？

00:02:54.659 --> 00:02:55.500
Is that what you call it?
你就是这么称呼它的吗？

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.500
like he's gone into the corner office.
就像他走进了角落的办公室一样。

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.699
So I think that's what inspired this episode.
所以我认为这就是这一集的灵感来源。

00:03:00.699 --> 00:03:06.259
I think this was a more of a Carter episode than me, I believe, and it's why my memory of it isn't that strong either, Josh.
我认为这更像是卡特的插曲，而不是我相信的，这就是为什么我对它的记忆也不那么强烈，乔什。

00:03:06.259 --> 00:03:09.900
I was like really forgetting what happened in this episode at times.
有时我真的忘记了这一集中发生的事情。

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.180
It was fun to rediscover.
重新发现很有趣。

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.740
It's kind of like a deep cut this one.
这有点像一个深切口。

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.139
It was not a single on the album.
这不是专辑中的单曲。

00:03:15.139 --> 00:03:17.780
It is like a B side, but it's good.
虽然有点像B面，但是也不错。

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:24.259
But I remember where it came from for Carter and the room, you know, we were always two rooms.
但我记得卡特和房间的来源，你知道，我们总是两个房间。

00:03:24.259 --> 00:03:28.580
the writers rooms were always like in a room and a B room and that wasn't on this one.
编剧的房间总是像一个房间和一个B房间，但这个房间没有。

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:34.659
But I think it came from this very true thing Carter and I were wrestling with, which is we were now the bosses.
但我认为这来自卡特和我正在努力解决的一个非常真实的事情，那就是我们现在是老板。

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:43.860
We had been staff writers on all of these shows, wrote for David Letterman, we were on American Dad, we were on various and various other ones that no one we can't even prove exist anymore.
我们曾是所有这些节目的特约编剧，为大卫·莱特曼撰稿，我们在《美国爸爸》中，我们在各种各样的其他节目中，我们甚至无法证明任何人的存在。

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:48.180
And we had always been guys that were the employees, right?
我们一直都是员工，对吧？

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:53.419
We'd always been like around that water cooler, you know, maybe talking a little shit about the boss here and there, whatever it was.
我们总是像在饮水机周围一样，你知道，也许到处谈论老板，不管是什么。

00:03:53.419 --> 00:03:54.740
And now we were the boss.
现在我们是老板。

00:03:54.740 --> 00:04:07.180
And I think the emotional truth, the kernel of truth, kernel of truth, kernel of truth, here is, it was actually Carter processing this very real thing he and I were feeling about, what does it mean to now be the boss?
我认为情感真相，真相的核心，真相的核心，真相的核心，这实际上是卡特在处理他和我所感受到的这个非常真实的事情，现在成为老板意味着什么？

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:12.379
We're in season two, we really are the boss, this show seems to be continuing and it's weird, it's different.
我们已经进入第二季了，我们真的是老大，这个节目似乎还在继续，这很奇怪，很不同。

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:16.899
And you guys didn't even, relatively speaking, have that much time not being bosses.
相对而言，你们甚至没有那么多时间不做老板。

00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:20.459
Like you weren't like staffed for years and then suddenly you found yourself in this position.
就像你已经多年没有工作人员一样，然后突然你发现自己处于这个位置。

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:25.160
Like you were, you had a catapulted into this position by way of writing a great pilot.
就像你一样，你通过写一个伟大的试播集而一跃成为这个职位。

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:29.060
Yeah, and you don't, and there's no training for it really.
是的，你不知道，而且也没有真正的培训。

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:29.899
Right, right.
对，对。

00:04:29.899 --> 00:04:31.139
And it is really weird and hard.
这真的很奇怪而且很难。

00:04:31.139 --> 00:04:39.459
Like that scene, this truest scene for me rewatching it was when Ted comes back into that break room and realizes he's probably talking shit about his shirt now.
就像那个场景一样，对我来说最真实的场景是当特德回到休息室并意识到他现在可能在谈论他的衬衫时。

00:04:39.459 --> 00:04:40.300
Yeah.
是的。

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:41.579
That feeling of distance from.
那种距离感。

00:04:41.579 --> 00:04:42.420
Yeah.
是的。

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:43.259
That was so great.
那真是太棒了。

00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:44.620
Frime and camera trickery.
框架和相机的诡计。

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:46.379
I loved how it was kind of a wonder.
我喜欢这真是一个奇迹。

00:04:46.379 --> 00:04:48.379
It was a like clearly time had passed.
时间似乎已经过去了。

00:04:48.379 --> 00:04:49.939
I mean, that was really smart.
我的意思是，那真的很聪明。

00:04:49.939 --> 00:04:52.740
You know, I can relate to this on some level, Craig.
你知道，我在某种程度上可以理解这一点，克雷格。

00:04:52.740 --> 00:05:06.459
Like, you know how they talk about like, there's healthy uses of gossip, like there's a kind of bonding social cohesion that is like ancient in the village to kind of gossip.
就像，你知道他们是如何谈论的，八卦有健康的用途，就像有一种社会凝聚力，就像村里古老的八卦一样。

00:05:06.459 --> 00:05:12.699
Now, obviously gossip can get toxic and we all know that feeling of like, I've said too much and I feel terrible about myself.
现在，显然八卦会变得有毒，我们都知道那种感觉，就像我说得太多，我对自己感觉很糟糕。

00:05:13.819 --> 00:05:19.699
But it does provide a kind of, yeah, like a social bonding.
但它确实提供了一种，是的，就像社会联系一样。

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:25.740
And I had a similar experience once, how I met your mother, got on the air, and started to get very successful.
我曾经有过类似的经历，我是如何认识你母亲的，然后开始做广播节目，然后开始变得非常成功。

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.379
I suddenly couldn't commiserate in the same way with my young actor.
我突然无法以同样的方式同情我的年轻演员。

00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:30.220
Yeah.
是的。

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:31.060
Yeah.
是的。

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:31.899
You can't complain.
你不能抱怨。

00:05:31.899 --> 00:05:33.540
You're not allowed to complain anymore.
你不被允许再抱怨了。

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:34.860
And those are like human beings who haven't.
这些人就像没有经历过的人一样。

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:39.220
Any listener of this podcast will know there was enough to complain about that was real.
这个播客的任何听众都会知道有足够多的抱怨是真实的。

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:42.139
But I could only, at some point I could only complain to you.
但我只能,有的时候我只能向你诉苦。

00:05:42.139 --> 00:05:42.980
Yeah.
是的。

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:43.819
Or Kobe.
或者科比。

00:05:43.819 --> 00:05:52.500
I couldn't complain to my friends that I went to drama school with or friends of mine from New York who were still really struggling and make ends meet.
我不能向我的朋友们抱怨我和我一起上戏剧学校的朋友，或者我来自纽约的朋友们，他们仍然在挣扎着维持生计。

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.699
And it puts you in a somewhat lonelier strategy.
这会让你陷入一种更加孤独的策略。

00:05:56.699 --> 00:05:58.540
It's a very lonely.
这是一种非常孤独的感觉。

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:01.379
Jordiana and I just watched Noah Bombox, new movie, Jay Kelly.
乔丹娜和我刚刚看了诺亚炸弹箱，新电影杰·凯利。

00:06:01.379 --> 00:06:02.300
Did you watch it?
你看了吗？

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.139
I haven't seen it yet.
我还没看到呢。

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:15.139
Okay, but you know, it's about like the loneliness of stardom, the loneliness of a guy who really chose the love of kind of anonymous crowds over his own family.
好吧，但你知道，这就像明星的孤独，一个真正选择匿名人群的爱而不是自己的家庭的人的孤独。

00:06:15.139 --> 00:06:17.980
You know, like it's a really, it's a tragic tale.
你知道，这就像一个真实的、悲惨的故事。

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:25.879
And not to say that I feel analogously, but that shift is very real.
并不是说我有类似的感觉，但这种转变是非常真实的。

00:06:25.879 --> 00:06:36.519
And there's, I don't know, there's something about, there's joy in having friends with which to have a like common enemy to bitch about.
而且，我不知道，有一些东西，有朋友，有共同的敌人可以互相抱怨，这是一种快乐。

00:06:36.519 --> 00:06:37.800
Yes, yes.
是的，是的。

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.160
And suddenly you're the common enemy they're bitching about.
突然之间，你就成了他们抱怨的共同敌人。

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:42.000
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:50.399
And Carter and I worked for, we have definitely in our career worked for show runners or head writers that seemed under immense stress.
卡特和我工作过，在我们的职业生涯中，我们肯定曾为似乎承受着巨大压力的节目主持人或首席编剧工作过。

00:06:50.399 --> 00:06:52.480
They were so low, flying solo, I mean.
我的意思是，它们是如此之低，独自飞行。

00:06:53.839 --> 00:07:00.160
And sometimes we're, you know, lousy bosses at times because of the stress they were under.
有时，你知道，我们有时会因为压力而成为糟糕的老板。

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:07.160
And I'm very grateful to have had Carter to share that loneliness with, to share the shittiness.
我非常感激能有卡特来分享我的孤独，分享我的糟糕。

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:13.959
And so people can complain about it either of us, but no, it made me remember that at least we had that.
所以人们可以抱怨我们任何一个人，但不，这让我记得至少我们有过这样的经历。

00:07:13.959 --> 00:07:19.360
We definitely felt cut off from the thing of like the group, the group that is working for the man.
我们确实感到与这个为这个人工作的团队之类的事情隔绝了。

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:20.759
We're now the man.
我们现在是男人了。

00:07:20.759 --> 00:07:23.839
We're the man and it's like, it is weirdly lonely.
我们是男人，感觉很奇怪的孤独。

00:07:23.839 --> 00:07:32.860
Well, if I could pay you guys a compliment, I do feel like you threaded a needle in terms of, I never, and again, I wasn't in the writer's room.
好吧，如果我能给你们点赞的话，我确实觉得你们在说，我从来没有，再说一次，我不在作家的房间里。

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:36.839
I never got the feeling the writers had resentment towards you.
我从来没有感觉到编剧们对你有怨恨。

00:07:36.839 --> 00:07:51.519
You all felt like a team, even though the final word would be you guys, I'm sure you grabbed certain scripts and did rewrites that, you know, but that's, if you're a comedy writer in Hollywood, you get used to that, but I never felt that.
你们都感觉像一个团队，尽管最后的决定权是你们，我相信你们抓住了某些剧本并重写了它，你知道，但那是，如果你是好莱坞的喜剧作家，你会习惯这一点，但我从未有过这种感觉。

00:07:51.519 --> 00:08:00.839
I do, I am curious though, looking back all these years later, what would you feel comfortable revealing, like what were some rookie mistakes you made as a boss?
我确实这么做了，但我很好奇，这么多年过去了，你会愿意透露哪些内容，比如你作为老板犯过哪些菜鸟错误？

00:08:00.839 --> 00:08:04.279
Yeah, I mean, a lot of mistakes.
是的，我的意思是，有很多错误。

00:08:04.279 --> 00:08:11.720
We, I think didn't realize that part of the job is teaching other people how to do the job the way you want to.
我认为我们没有意识到工作的一部分是教其他人如何按照你想要的方式完成工作。

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:19.519
Sometimes we would, if there was something wrong with a script in early seasons, we'd just be like, All right, let's lock ourselves in our office, barricade the doors and we'll fix it.
有时候，如果前几季的剧本出了问题，我们就会说，好吧，让我们把自己锁在办公室里，把门堵上，我们会修复它。

00:08:19.519 --> 00:08:23.199
And sometimes you need to invest in fixing it with everybody.
有时你需要投资与每个人一起修复它。

00:08:23.199 --> 00:08:25.040
And learn how to delegate.
并学习如何授权。

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.600
Yeah, and there were times where we didn't do enough of that.
是的，有时我们做得还不够。

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:30.560
And then we really leaned into doing that.
然后我们真的倾向于这样做。

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:31.920
And we really empowered people.
我们确实赋予了人们权力。

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.039
And we would bring writers, even if we barricade the door, we'd bring a couple of other writers in it.
我们会带来作家，即使我们把门堵住，我们也会带来其他几个作家。

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:37.960
And we could say, this is what we're trying to fix.
我们可以说，这就是我们正在努力解决的问题。

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:41.039
It's hard to do it as a group of eight people in a room.
八个人在一个房间里很难做到这一点。

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:42.600
We just need to go really fast right now.
我们现在只需要走得很快。

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.480
So it's you and Carter and me.
所以是你、卡特和我。

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:49.639
and we're gonna fix it and we're gonna sort of teach you what we think needs to be better in the script.
我们会解决这个问题，我们会告诉你我们认为剧本中需要改进的地方。

